course: dictionary
lesson: 8508
title: Same word, different meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words change their meaning depending on where you find them. Context, the words and sentences around a word, is your guide. «The satanic leaf-tailed gecko has a body and tail that look like a dry curled leaf.» This sentence is about a lizard, so satanic is part of the animal's name here, not a description of wickedness.
explain: Satanic has three main meanings. It can mean to do with Satan, who Christians believe is the devil. It can mean very evil or cruel, as in a story about a wicked villain. In literature, William Blake's phrase «dark satanic mills» is often taken to mean the grim factories of the Industrial Revolution.
explain: To choose the right meaning, ask: what is the sentence about? Then try each meaning in the sentence. Does "a satanic gecko that is very cruel" make sense for a leaf-shaped lizard? It does not fit, so the clues tell you satanic is part of a name.
fact: «Satanic» means to do with Satan, who Christians believe is the devil, the chief spirit of evil.
fact: «Satanic» can also mean very evil or cruel.
fact: «Dark satanic mills» is a phrase from William Blake's poem «Jerusalem», often taken to mean the grim factories of the Industrial Revolution.
fact: In religion lessons, Satan is described as a fallen angel who tempts people to do wrong.
fact: The same word can have different meanings, so always check the context before you decide.
exercise: «The satanic leaf-tailed gecko has a body and tail that look like a dry curled leaf.» In this sentence, what is satanic part of? | A dry curled leaf | *A name for an animal | A type of factory | A spell cast by a witch
exercise: Mrs Clark says, «The satanic forces in the story were cruel and wicked.» What does satanic mean here? | Made of leaves | *Very evil or cruel | To do with the factories of the Industrial Revolution | Happy and kind
exercise: Which phrase comes from a famous poem by William Blake? | satanic laugh | satanic forces | satanic leaf-tailed gecko | *dark satanic mills
test: Christians believe Satan is what? | A kind of gecko | An angel who never does wrong | A type of factory | *The devil, the chief spirit of evil
test: In the phrase «dark satanic mills», what are the mills often taken to mean? | Schools for young children | Gardens full of flowers | Places where lizards live | *The grim factories of the Industrial Revolution
exam: What does satanic mean in «The satanic leaf-tailed gecko has no eyelids and licks its eyes clean»? | To do with Satan, the devil | *Part of the name of a lizard, not evil | A dark, grim factory | Very cruel, like a villain
